>
>PIII 1.26 GHz
>512 MB RAM
>Sager Laptop
>
>If more info is needed, please specify.

It's hard to know where to begin. Fan activity is *usually* a BIOS issue, 
not an OS issue. So perhaps the place to start is by asking you why you 
think the Linux install has anything to do with this problem. And, for that 
matter, why you believe it is a problem at all (see below).

Although laptops can be funky, with hardware-specific extensions to their 
as-shipped OSs, the Sager Web site seems to indicate that the base 
configurations ship with no OS installed (and only Windows choices are 
mentioned as options) ... so I wouldn't expect something as fundamental to 
hardware health as fan operation to depend on specifis of the hardware 
configuration.

In laptops, fans are (usually) not always on. Is it possible that the 
system is simply running at a light enough load that it is not getting hot 
enough to cause the fan to start? The discussion here -- 
http://www.ischo.com/notebook/ -- the destination of a link to info about a 
"Sager 6500" at http://www.linux.org/hardware/laptop.html (even though the 
actual text does not mention Sager) -- seems to indicate that it may be 
that simple ... thus a "feature", not a problem.

Perhaps you will get lucky and find someone here who is familiar with your 
hardware, but I wouldn't bet on it. So if you post again, please identify 
the model of your laptop a bit more exactly (I couldn't find anything that 
*quite* matched the specs you give at the Sager Web site).



